  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Login Mechanisms**\n\nOkay, I\'m now digging into how native forms and `fetch` calls interact with the login API endpoint. I\'m scrutinizing `LoginPage.tsx`, `HomePage.tsx`, and `RegisterPage.tsx`, along with the `server.ts` file, and the `/src/server` directory to grasp the request handling and routing.\n\n\n**Analyzing Cookie Attributes**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the cookie attributes in `/auth.ts`, specifically how `sameSite: \'lax\'` interacts with cross-site iframes in the AI Studio preview. I\'ve confirmed that this setting is being blocked in Chrome, Safari, and Firefox. It\'s a key issue, especially in a Cloud Run environment like `ais-dev-...run.app` embedded within `aistudio.google.com`. The browser ignores `SameSite=Lax` cookies in fetch requests. I\'m exploring solutions to this cross-site restriction.\n\n\n**Addressing Cookie & Navigation**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that changing `sameSite` and `secure` fixes cookie storage within the AI Studio iframe. Now, I\'m focusing on the navigation issues. I\'ve pinpointed the problem in `App.tsx` where `onRefreshUser()` wasn\'t being awaited, leading to immediate redirects. I\'m implementing `await onRefreshUser()` before navigation to ensure the user data is loaded before redirecting. I\'m also now thinking about browsers that block third-party cookies altogether.\n\n\n**Considering Third-Party Cookie Handling**\n\nI\'m now contemplating the implications of third-party cookie blocking within the iframe environment. If a login returns success but the `httpOnly` cookie isn\'t set due to restrictions, should `App.tsx` use a session token or `sessionStorage` fallback? I am thinking of how this interacts with `onRefreshUser()` and the user data. I\'m exploring an approach using a secure `httpOnly` cookie paired with a fallback mechanism for restricted browsers.\n\n\n**Refining Fetch & Cookies**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on how `fetch` requests behave when cookies are unavailable, specifically concerning `/api/quotas/history`, `/api/profile`, and others. My understanding is that these requests will fail. I\'m exploring the use of an `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header as a fallback if cookies are blocked. Crucially, I\'m keeping the session token within the `httpOnly` cookie. The key is to ensure the solution adheres to the requirement: sessions in a dedicated table, with a random token in an `httpOnly`, `SameSite=lax` cookie. The ideal fix involves `SameSite=None; Secure` on HTTPS combined with `trust proxy`. Failing that, I must craft an explicit message.\n\n\n"
