  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Diagnosing Core Issues**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed three key issues with complete certainty. The first is the un-awaited `onRefreshUser()` function calls in `HomePage.tsx`, `LoginPage.tsx`, and `RegisterPage.tsx`. This causes a race condition. I\'ve noted the specific line numbers to aid in remediation. I\'m focusing on the implications of this async behaviour.\n\n\n**Analyzing Cookie Issues**\n\nI\'ve determined that the `SameSite=Lax` setting for the `lpcs_session` cookie is problematic within the AI Studio iframe due to cross-site cookie restrictions. The browser is rejecting the cookie in this case. I\'ve formulated a solution: when the request is secure, I must set `sameSite: \'none\'` and `secure: true`; otherwise, it\'s `lax` and `false`. I also propose setting `app.set(\'trust proxy\', 1)`. I\'m now considering the implications of third-party cookie blocking within the iframe.\n\n\n**Evaluating Cookie Strategies**\n\nI\'m now exploring scenarios where the browser blocks `SameSite=None; Secure` cookies within the iframe. If this happens, I could check `onRefreshUser()` immediately after the login fetch, to be certain. Better yet, if we can validate a fallback session, it may still not solve the problem! If Chrome Incognito blocks ALL third-party cookies, even `SameSite=None; Secure` won\'t be sent to `/api/auth/me` . I\'m working to make the cookie primary and the token a backup in case the cookie fails.\n\n\n**Addressing Third-Party Restrictions**\n\nI\'m now contemplating the implications of third-party cookie restrictions, especially within an iframe context. My latest idea involves testing if `SameSite=None; Secure` cookies are functional. If `onRefreshUser()` returns null, indicating cookie blocking, I will display a clear message in Portuguese with a button to open the app in a new tab. I also need to consider how to handle situations where even that approach is blocked in incognito or Safari. I\'m investigating how other apps handle iframe sessions.\n\n\n**Refining Cookie & Token Strategy**\n\nI\'m solidifying my approach. Using `SameSite` settings, `httpOnly`, and secure cookies, with a short-lived or session token in `sessionStorage` as a fallback when cookies are blocked in the iframe, should make this robust. Checking `onRefreshUser()` immediately after login will provide certainty regarding cookie availability. If the cookie is blocked, I can now display a clear, concise message in Portuguese, directing the user to open the app in a new tab. This seems like a promising strategy.\n\n\n**Implementing Incognito Login**\n\nMy current thinking revolves around improving the login process, even in restrictive Incognito iframe environments. I\'m exploring an approach to make this work. I plan to check whether we can make it work inside an Incognito iframe by patching window.fetch when the cookie is failing. I need to make login work by sending the `user` object from `/api/auth/login`. If this still works inside the iframe, the cookie is not blocked and there is no need to patch window.fetch. I am also planning on refactoring `trust proxy` in `server.ts` and `createSession` and `clearSession` in `src/server/auth.ts` to use `sameSite` settings.\n\n\n"
